# Chapter Eight — What She Kept

= Draft: dramatic conversation cut 0.2. The authored order visits Lydia's apartment before Paul's room; a later playable pass may permit either order. The working crime engine remains unratified. No interaction branches yet.

## Scene 18 — Who is admitted

= Present: Henry, Nell, Lydia, Marian, and Robert.

= Immediate pressure: Lydia agreed to admit Nell and Henry under conditions. Nell will not let her choose the witnesses again.

INT. LYDIA'S APARTMENT, WESTLAKE - AFTERNOON

At eight minutes after one, Nell and Henry reach Lydia's second-floor apartment in Westlake. The door is open. A sealed carton sits in the hall with PORTLAND written twice across the top.

Ruth surrendered Paul's room key at headquarters at eleven and is still there giving a statement. She will meet them later at the Kenton Rooms.

Robert comes out carrying an electric kettle wrapped in the morning newspaper.

ROBERT
You're here.

NELL
She said one.

ROBERT
She began at noon.

From inside the apartment:

LYDIA
I did not begin.

Marian appears behind Robert with a marking pencil. She takes the kettle from him, finds the carton marked KITCHEN, and writes OPEN FIRST beneath Lydia's hand.

MARIAN
She opened a closet.

LYDIA
To find the box.

MARIAN
That is beginning.

Nell steps inside. Henry waits in the hall until Lydia looks at him.

LYDIA
You may come in, Mr. Calder.

HENRY
Thank you.

The apartment is attractive in the way a room can be after its owner has removed every explanation. Pale rectangles remain where pictures hung. Books are packed spine down. One lamp and three chairs remain in use. Lydia's two suitcases stand beside a railway folder near the door.

NELL
Did you cancel the train?

LYDIA
I missed it.

NELL
Those are not the same thing.

LYDIA
There are other trains.

MARIAN
She has checked.

Lydia turns to Marian.

LYDIA
You were not invited to this.

MARIAN
Nell asked me.

ROBERT
You asked me to finish the kitchen.

LYDIA
I know why you are here.

NELL
Then we can begin.

Lydia closes the apartment door.

LYDIA
One box. I open it. Nothing leaves this room.

HENRY
If anything appears connected to Paul's death, I call Ward and it stays where it is.

LYDIA
That is what I said.

MARIAN
No. You said it belonged to you.

Lydia looks at her.

MARIAN
You did not use the words. I heard them anyway.

Henry takes out his notebook.

LYDIA
You may write. Do not open the desk, the suitcases, or any box I do not put on the table.

HENRY
Agreed.

Lydia goes to the kitchen doorway.

LYDIA
Would anyone like coffee?

ROBERT
The pot is in six.

Marian looks at him.

ROBERT
The carton. Number six.

MARIAN
When did you pack the pot?

ROBERT
Tuesday.

MARIAN
You said you came Tuesday to move the bookcase.

ROBERT
I moved the bookcase.

Lydia takes five glasses from the drainboard and fills them at the tap. She gives one to each person. Nobody had asked for water.

From beneath the dining table, she pulls a deep wardrobe carton. It is large enough to hold folded cloth, not the shallow document carton Henry saw on the gallery at Tolland's.

A store transfer tag is tied through one handle.

Lydia puts the carton on the table.

## Scene 19 — Private Weather

= Present: Henry, Nell, Lydia, Marian, and Robert.

= Immediate pressure: Nell wants to recognize her mother before Lydia and Marian turn June into two incompatible explanations.

INT. LYDIA'S APARTMENT - AFTERNOON

Lydia cuts the old string around the carton. The lid lifts without resistance.

ROBERT
I thought there'd be film.

Nobody answers him. There is no film.

On top lies a black ledger with linen tape along its spine. Beneath it are two contact sheets, several production stills, a worn envelope, and a packet of carbon pages held with a rusted clip.

Lydia places each item on the table in the order she removes it. She empties the worn envelope last. It contains three call sheets, an old index card, and a pink Tolland's telephone message slip dated three weeks earlier.

Lydia places the ledger in front of Nell.

Stamped on the cover:

!PRIVATE WEATHER

!CONTINUITY / WARDROBE — L. SHAW

!ARTHUR RUSK, DIRECTOR

!1958

Henry reads the director's name twice.

HENRY
Arthur Rusk directed this?

LYDIA
For nine days.

MARIAN
Eleven.

ROBERT
The set stood for eleven. They photographed for nine.

NELL
My mother said she worked on a picture once.

LYDIA
She did.

NELL
She said she helped with a dress.

MARIAN
That is how it began.

NELL
What began?

Lydia turns one contact sheet toward Nell. Twelve small black-and-white frames show the same bedroom from slightly different angles. In several, a woman stands beside a wardrobe with one hand on its closed door.

Nell bends closer.

NELL
That is her.

No one confirms it. No one needs to.

In the next frame June has turned toward someone outside the picture. Her mouth is open. In the frame after that she is laughing and the slate has lowered into view.

!PRIVATE WEATHER — SCENE 41 — TAKE 3.

NELL
Was she acting?

LYDIA
Yes.

MARIAN
She was working.

ROBERT
She was cold.

Nell looks at him.

ROBERT
They had no heat on the stage. She came home in that coat every night.

NELL
Was the dress hers?

LYDIA
The coat was. I made the dress for the picture.

NELL
Was she paid?

LYDIA
For the days she performed.

NELL
Only that?

Lydia opens the ledger.

The early pages are measurements, fabric samples, and notes about which sleeve June used to open the wardrobe. Farther in, Lydia's handwriting begins to share the margins with another hand.

Nell touches the air above one blue-pencil note without touching the page.

NELL
That is my mother's writing.

HENRY
You're certain?

NELL
She made her capital J backward first and corrected it. Every time.

The note begins with a corrected J.

Beside it Lydia had written: JUNE — REHEARSAL. KEEP FOR TOMORROW.

NELL
What did “keep” mean?

LYDIA
Rusk wanted the movement in the next day's pages.

NELL
What movement?

LYDIA
June closed the wardrobe before she answered the man in the hall. In the script she answered first.

MARIAN
She said it felt false.

LYDIA
She said a woman would close the door before she lied.

NELL
Was she right?

LYDIA
They changed it.

NELL
That was not what I asked.

Lydia does not answer.

Henry turns one page by its corner only after Lydia nods. A typed slug appears above the continuity grid:

!INT. BEDROOM — NIGHT

HENRY
This heading was on the paper in Paul's hand.

Robert puts down his glass.

MARIAN
The same words?

HENRY
The same scene heading. I could not see the rest.

Near the bottom of the page, in June's blue pencil, is a sentence Lydia repeated at supper:

!DON'T IMPROVE IT JUST BECAUSE IT'S OVER.

Nell reads it once without speaking.

Henry closes his notebook.

HENRY
This connects the archive to the paper with Paul. I need to call Ward now.

Lydia points to the telephone on a small table beside the hall. Henry dials, waits through a transfer, and tells Ward in four sentences what is on the table and whose hand is on it. He listens, says "Nothing has left the table," and hangs up.

HENRY
He is sending an officer. Nothing else is handled until someone records the order. We may keep talking.

He comes back to the table.

HENRY
Was that written for the scene?

LYDIA
Not at first.

MARIAN
June said it at home.

LYDIA
She said it during rehearsal.

MARIAN
The rehearsal was in her home.

NELL
Did she write the picture?

LYDIA
She wrote that.

MARIAN
She said it.

LYDIA
Rusk put it in the pages the next morning.

NELL
Under whose name?

Nobody answers.

Nell looks again at June beside the wardrobe.

NELL
Was she good?

Lydia looks at the contact sheet as if it might permit an answer.

MARIAN
I don't know.

Nell turns to her.

MARIAN
I knew when she was tired. I knew when she was pretending not to be angry. I did not know what the camera saw.

ROBERT
She practiced that scene until all of us knew when to close a cupboard.

NELL
That is not the same as good.

ROBERT
No.

The old index card on the table lists six rehearsal reels and a blue folder of dialogue pages in Lydia's hand. Each entry is marked RETURNED TO JUNE. None of the objects is in the carton.

Marian reads the first line from where she stands.

MARIAN
Where are the recordings?

Lydia looks up.

LYDIA
You know they are not here.

## Scene 20 — For Nell

= Present: Henry, Nell, Lydia, Marian, and Robert.

= Immediate pressure: Preservation and destruction have both been performed in Nell's name. She wants one of the people responsible to admit that neither asked her.

INT. LYDIA'S APARTMENT - AFTERNOON

MARIAN
I asked you once.

LYDIA
Twice.

MARIAN
The second time was about the recordings.

LYDIA
You said all of it.

NELL
Asked her what?

Marian looks at the open carton.

MARIAN
To get rid of it.

NELL
When?

MARIAN
After June died.

LYDIA
And three weeks ago.

Nell waits.

MARIAN
Yes.

NELL
Why?

MARIAN
Those tapes were not a performance. Not all of them. She stopped, started again, complained about the room. She spoke before she knew which parts men would call the picture.

LYDIA
That is why they mattered.

MARIAN
That is why they were hers.

NELL
So you wanted them destroyed.

MARIAN
I wanted them left with her.

NELL
She was dead.

MARIAN
I know.

The words come out louder than Marian intended. Robert takes one step toward her. She shakes her head and he stops.

MARIAN
People had already called it improvisation when they did not want to pay her. I did not want them finding her voice after she could no longer tell them no.

LYDIA
Destroying the record would have helped them.

MARIAN
Keeping it in your workroom for fourteen years helped whom?

Lydia's hand moves toward the ledger.

NELL
No.

Lydia leaves it open.

NELL
You kept it for me. Marian wanted it destroyed for me. When were either of you going to ask me?

LYDIA
You were fifteen.

NELL
I was twenty-nine last month.

LYDIA
I was going to tell you.

NELL
Last night?

LYDIA
Yes.

NELL
With Paul at the table and a train in the morning.

LYDIA
I was leaving.

NELL
That is why you should have told me sooner.

Marian sits in the chair nearest the table.

Henry points to the pink message slip on the table.

HENRY
Why three weeks ago?

The slip has four entries in Lydia's hand:

!CALLER: M.H.

418-R / PRIVATE WEATHER / BEDROOM / WARDROBE BEFORE LINE?

The space for a return number is blank.

MARIAN
M.H.

NELL
Not you.

MARIAN
I have never telephoned Lydia at that store in my life. I go in.

LYDIA
That is true. A woman telephoned me at Tolland's. She gave me the initials. She said she was checking an old scene for a picture in production.

HENRY
Did she name the new picture?

LYDIA
No.

HENRY
Did she name Rusk?

LYDIA
No. She knew the bedroom, the wardrobe, and June's line.

NELL
How did you know the recordings had been in our apartment?

LYDIA
Marian and I packed them after June died. The reels and blue folder went into a box marked JUNE — WORK.

MARIAN
It stayed in my garage until you and Paul moved.

NELL
I took all the boxes from the garage.

LYDIA
After Paul left, I came to see you. The shelf where you kept June's box was empty. You said Paul had taken two boxes of papers.

NELL
I didn't know what was in them.

LYDIA
I did.

NELL
For how long?

LYDIA
For years.

NELL
And last night was the first time you thought to tell me.

LYDIA
Last night was the first time he agreed to bring them back.

Henry writes *418-R* beneath his earlier notes. The apartment buzzer has not yet sounded.

## Scene 21 — Robert's part

= Present: Henry, Nell, Lydia, Marian, and Robert.

= Immediate pressure: Lydia has taken responsibility in the form she prefers. Robert must decide whether to let her make his part disappear inside it.

INT. LYDIA'S APARTMENT - AFTERNOON

Nell looks from the message slip to the index card.

NELL
How did you reach Paul?

LYDIA
He telephoned.

NELL
Here?

ROBERT
He called me.

Marian turns toward her husband.

ROBERT
Paul telephoned me in June. He asked whether Nell still lived at the old address. I did not tell him. I took his number.

NELL
Why did he call you?

ROBERT
I don't know. He had the house number. We've had it since we came to the house, Marian wanted it changed when the—it doesn't matter. He knew I would answer. That's all it was. I answer the telephone. I'm sorry.

NELL
Did you tell Lydia?

ROBERT
Yes.

MARIAN
Did you tell me?

ROBERT
No.

MARIAN
I wanted you to say no.

ROBERT
I can't.

MARIAN
No.

Robert looks at the floor.

HENRY
Did you go to the Kenton Rooms?

ROBERT
Twice.

MARIAN
When?

ROBERT
Last week with Lydia's list. Yesterday afternoon, after Paul said he had found the things on it. I took him a sandwich. I don't know why I'm telling you that. He hadn't eaten.

NELL
Did you see them?

ROBERT
I saw the two boxes from your apartment. I saw the blue folder on the bed. There were small tape reels. Paul said he would put everything from the list into a separate carton and bring it to you.

HENRY
Did you help him pack that carton?

ROBERT
No. I did not touch it. At dinner I heard him tell Edwin he had brought it.

MARIAN
You saw him twice. You came here to pack. You met Lydia without telling me.

ROBERT
Yes.

MARIAN
Was there anything you did tell me?

Robert begins to answer. His hand has gone to the transfer tag tied to the deep carton.

Henry looks at it.

The printed heading reads TOLLAND'S INTERNAL TRANSFER. Beneath it, DISCARDED PATTERNS has been written over PRIVATE PROPERTY. E. PRICE appears in the authorization box.

HENRY
Did you move this carton too?

ROBERT
Yes.

MARIAN
When you moved the bookcase.

ROBERT
The same day.

MARIAN
From where?

ROBERT
The alterations workroom at Tolland's. Lydia had kept it there.

HENRY
Edwin authorized the transfer.

ROBERT
Yes.

HENRY
Why does it say “discarded patterns”?

ROBERT
You will have to ask Edwin.

MARIAN
You told me you were helping Lydia pack.

ROBERT
I was.

The answer remains in the room for a moment.

ROBERT
I knew what you wanted done with the box.

MARIAN
So you lied to me.

ROBERT
Yes.

MARIAN
For Lydia.

ROBERT
No.

He looks at Nell.

ROBERT
I thought Nell should decide.

NELL
You did not tell me either.

ROBERT
No.

Marian removes the marking pencil from behind her ear and puts it on the table.

NELL
Why put all of us at supper?

Lydia's hand rests on the back of the one empty chair.

LYDIA
Paul would not return the material without speaking to you. You would not agree to see him if I told you why. Ruth had helped him leave and I wanted her to hear him say what he had done. I am not proud of the method. I am not ashamed of it either. When you are the only one left who remembers how a thing began, you stop waiting for the others to—

NELL
You did not tell any of us.

LYDIA
You might not have come.

NELL
That was our answer.

LYDIA
Yes.

Lydia grips the chair, then releases it.

LYDIA
I was trying to finish this before I left.

NELL
It was not yours to finish.

No one answers.

Henry writes four things in his notebook: *Private Weather*. *Arthur Rusk*. *418-R*. *Six reels and blue folder absent*.

At the top of the open carton, the contact sheet has shifted. One frame shows June between takes, looking away from the camera while someone outside the frame speaks.

Lydia reaches to put the ledger over it.

Marian reaches for Lydia's wrist before the ledger comes down.

NELL
Leave it.

Both women stop.
